EC2 Pricing / Purchasing Options

On-Demand

Pay for what you use
No long-term commitment
Most flexible option
More expensive than Reserved Instances / Savings Plans
Good for short-term, unpredictable workloads

Reserved Instances

Commit to 1 or 3 years
Lower cost than On-Demand
Good for predictable, steady workloads
Less flexible than On-Demand

Savings Plans

Commit to a certain amount of usage per hour for 1 or 3 years
Lower cost than On-Demand
More flexible than Reserved Instances
Good for predictable workloads while retaining some flexibility

Spot Instances

Can provide large discounts compared to On-Demand
AWS can interrupt the instance when capacity is needed
2-minute interruption notice
Best for fault-tolerant and flexible workloads
Batch processing
Data analysis
Big data
Distributed workloads
CI/CD
Do not use when the workload cannot tolerate interruption

Spot Fleet

Collection of Spot Instances and optionally On-Demand Instances
Used to achieve a desired amount of compute capacity
Can use different instance types/AZs to improve availability and flexibility

Dedicated Hosts

Entire physical server dedicated to your organization
Useful for:
Software licensing requirements
Compliance requirements
Workloads requiring dedicated physical hardware
More expensive than normal EC2

Capacity Reservations

Reserve EC2 capacity in a specific Availability Zone
Charged at On-Demand rates
Used when you need to guarantee EC2 capacity will be available
Does not provide a discount
Important cost concepts

Lowest cost → Spot
Flexible / no commitment → On-Demand
Predictable workload + long-term commitment → Reserved Instances / Savings Plans
Guaranteed capacity in an AZ → Capacity Reservation
Dedicated physical server → Dedicated Hos
